+```python
+def maintenance_actions(self, hermes_home: str) -> list:
+    # Quick: it runs when the settings page opens. ready False, with status saying why, when it cannot run yet.
+    return [{"id": "index", "label": "Index facts for search by meaning",
+             "description": "Embeds every stored fact that has no vector yet.",
+             "status": "80 of 120 facts indexed", "ready": True}]
+
+def run_maintenance_step(self, action_id: str, hermes_home: str) -> dict:
+    # One bounded step (one batch); Hermes calls it again until done and prints each status.
+    ...
+    return {"done": False, "status": "112 of 120 facts indexed"}
+    # last: {"done": True, "status": "120 of 120 facts indexed", "result": "Indexed 40 facts in 18 s."}
+```
+
+Both run on an uninitialized instance outside any session, with `hermes_home` passed in, and take and return JSON
+values only, so they work under `plugins.isolation: host`. A step that raises fails the job with its message; one
+that keeps returning the same status 20 times in a row is stopped. Only one job runs at a time per backend.
+
+Job entries and step results can include a `translations` map keyed by Hermes language ID. Translate job
+`label`, `description`, and `status`, and step `status` and `result`. Hermes uses the profile's language and
+falls back to the original string for each untranslated field. Job IDs stay the same in every language.
+
+A job can ask for one choice before it runs, and it can propose a value for one of the provider's config fields:
+
+```python
+def maintenance_actions(self, hermes_home: str) -> list:
+    return [
+        # The user picks one option; "selected" is the one in use. A description may have several lines.
+        {"id": "deploy", "label": "Local model", "description": "...", "status": "", "ready": True,
+         "options": [{"id": "small", "label": "Small", "description": "Download: 92 MiB\nCached: 0 MiB"},
+                     {"id": "large", "label": "Large", "description": "Download: 2.14 GiB\nCached: 2.14 GiB"}],
+         "selected": "large"},
+        # Shown beside the "threshold" field, in the panel for an inline field and in the full config for the
+        # rest. After a run that found a value, "suggestion" carries it and
+        # "status" says what it rests on; the Desktop writes it to the field only when the user confirms.
+        {"id": "calibrate", "label": "Initialize threshold", "description": "...", "ready": True,
+         "field": "threshold", "suggestion": "0.61", "status": "Suggested 0.61: 30 of 30 probes recalled, ..."},
+    ]
+
+def run_maintenance_step(self, action_id: str, hermes_home: str, option: str = "") -> dict:
+    ...  # option: the picked option's id, passed only to a job that lists options
+```
+
+Option IDs follow the job ID rule and options translate `label` and `description`. `hermes memory action <id>
+--option <option>` runs a job with a choice; without `--option` it runs the selected one. A job never writes a
+suggested value itself: leave it out when there is too little to judge from, and say why in `status`.
+
 ## Config Schema
 
 `get_config_schema()` returns a list of field descriptors used by `hermes memory setup`:
@@ -428,6 +483,60 @@ def write_config_fields(self, values: dict, hermes_home: str) -> None:
 Declaring `storage=STORAGE_PROVIDER` without `write_config_fields` makes a save return 501; a
 provider that fails to load returns 503; an unknown `storage` value shows no panel and refuses saves.
 
+`ProviderConfigSchema` and `ProviderField` accept an optional `translations` map for display text.
+For example, `translations={"zh": {"label": "工作区", "description": "用于存放记忆的工作区。"}}`
+localizes a field in Simplified Chinese. The schema supports `label`, and fields support `label`,
+`description`, `info`, `placeholder`, and `group`. Keep keys and values stable across languages.
+
+For a provider with a connection wizard, declare `connection_fields` as a tuple of schema field keys and
+optionally `connection_job` as the ID of a maintenance job that sets up a local connection. Desktop shows them
+as a Cloud | Local choice beside the provider settings, each side with its own form, and marks the mode
+`read_config_fields` reports as `connection_state` (`"cloud"`, `"local"` or `""`). The full-config dialog shows
+only the fields that are neither `inline` nor reserved for the connection. Fields reserved for the connection are submitted to
+`connect_config_fields(values, hermes_home)` on the active provider. That method owns validation, synthetic
+probing and persistence. Preserve the previous connection when probing fails. Keep submitted secrets write-only
+and reuse a saved secret only for its intended endpoint.
+
+### Memory reads for displays and the checkup
+
+Optional methods let Hermes show what a provider stores and let the user check it. Each runs on an uninitialized instance,
+reads the profile at `hermes_home` and nothing else, changes nothing, makes no network call and
+returns JSON values. The default is `{}`: not supported, which the Desktop shows as "not reported",
+never as an empty store.
+
+Budget entries can also provide `translations` for `label` and `unit`.
+
+```python
+def usage_report(self, hermes_home: str) -> dict:
+    # Every key is optional. A budget is a limit the provider itself enforces, counted the way it
+    # enforces it; report no limit you do not enforce.
+    return {"items": 56, "storage_bytes": 180224, "budgets": [
+        {"key": "catalog", "label": "Fact catalog", "enabled": True, "used": 1170, "limit": 3000,
+         "unit": "estimated tokens", "listed": 54, "eligible": 56, "overlaps": ["memory"],
+         # optional: one line the capacity display shows as written (translate it under "translations")
+         "note": "3 longer facts have no keywords, so the catalog shows only how they open.",
+         # optional: the inline settings field that sets this limit
+         "field": "catalog_max_tokens"}]}
+
+def review_items(self, hermes_home: str) -> dict:
+    # What a memory checkup the user starts may read. Only id and text are required.
+    return {"items": [{"id": "42", "text": "The database is PostgreSQL 16", "created_at": 1767225600.0,
+                       "protected": False, "uses": 9, "score": 0.8, "unconfirmed": False}]}
+```
+
+`usage_report` feeds `layers.provider` of `GET /api/memory`. A budget whose text lists the stored items in
+every session (a generated catalog) may name the built-in files that then become optional in `overlaps`
+(`"memory"`, `"user"`): while that budget and the file are both on and the file holds something, the Desktop
+suggests turning the file off, with what it costs per session. It only suggests: nothing is switched off, and
+both may stay on. A budget that names the inline settings `field` holding its limit is shown under that field in
+the provider's settings, where the limit is edited, and not a second time in the summary above them; Command
+Center, which has no settings, still lists it. `review_items` feeds
+`POST /api/memory/checkup`: one model call with no tools that returns proposals and writes nothing.
+The provider only reports. Hermes orders the checkup by `score`, `unconfirmed` and `uses` (in that
+weight; low use alone never justifies a removal), and holds back any proposal to remove or change a
+`protected` item or to remove one created in the last seven days. Leave out of `review_items`
+whatever your provider would not put in front of a model (credentials, for one).
+
