+### How full memory is, in the desktop app
+
+**Settings → Memory & Context** and **Command Center → Maintenance** show how much each layer carries: `USER.md` and
+`MEMORY.md` against their character limits, and the active provider's store as a count and a size. A provider's store
+has no limit to fill, so it gets no percentage; a budget the provider itself enforces (a generated catalog, for
+example) is shown against that budget, by the provider's own estimate. A layer that is switched off, empty, unreadable
+or not reported says which.
+
+When the provider's catalog lists your stored facts in every session and `MEMORY.md` is on as well, the panel says
+that `MEMORY.md` has become optional and roughly how many tokens it adds to each session. Turning it off is up to
+you: nothing is switched off for you, and the two can stay on together; keeping both only means one more block in
+every session's context.
+
+### Memory checkup in the desktop app
+
+**Command Center → Maintenance → Memory checkup → Review now** (also reached from **Settings → Memory & Context →
+Open memory checkup**) asks your configured model to read those layers and propose what to update, merge or remove. It is one model call with no tools, so it cannot write: the result is a list
+of proposals, by layer, each naming its target, its evidence and what must be preserved. Tick the ones you approve and
+**Add selected to chat** puts them in the chat box as a request; Hermes makes them with the normal memory tools once you
+send it, under your `write_approval` setting. A checkup never proposes removing an entry you protected or a fact added
+in the last seven days, and rarely used is not a reason to remove anything: such suggestions are listed under **Needs
+evidence** instead.
+
+**Remind me to review** is on by default: it shows a reminder above the chat box every 10 or 14 days (14 by
+default, the first one 14 days after the app first opens the profile) with **Review now** and **Next time**. The
+model runs only when you choose **Review now**; turn the reminder off in the same panel and it stays off. The
+reminder is kept in this desktop installation, per connection and profile; it uses no cron job, no background model
+call and nothing in the system prompt, and **Next time** only dismisses it.
+
 ## Duplicate Prevention
 
 The memory system automatically rejects exact duplicate entries. If you try to add content that already exists, it returns success with a "no duplicate added" message.
